 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423668 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "047ee837-df71-5e5c-bcee-335a715a816d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "29e9d841-9401-5444-9a3a-2b082af4b7ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+u=x+v$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d091317-3092-5633-8a6e-4652d2fd3389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423670 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices $({a}_{ij})$  with real entries, such that  ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}$ is rational.  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87c89c45-3edb-5cd5-88f1-a3ee2b4d8003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423671 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because **multiplication by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\n For example, the identity matrix \n\n$$\nI=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nlies in $T$, but\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cdot I=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\notin T\n$$\n\nbecause $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2}$ is not a rational number.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $T.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is commutative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in T,$  the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in  $T,$ and   $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e561cf1-bbce-5b45-99d8-35b7ea403736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186399587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "467837ce-9aea-5bd2-99a0-3354bf395104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186423671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bbaac633-758e-54a8-bb41-3769e90b4fe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186423670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423675 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f72bd130-061d-5d95-9582-1d1554fa5cad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f97b0645-0a69-5367-a434-1456880e7389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423676 